---
import BaseLayout from '~/components/BaseLayout.astro';
import DocsShell from '~/components/DocsShell.astro';
import CodeBlock from '~/components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from '~/components/Callout.astro';
import { VERSIONS } from '~/data/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Teptris — TOML schema validation docs"
  description="JSON Schema 2020-12 validation for TOML documents, built into libteptris: compile once, validate many, from C, the CLI, Ruby, Python, JavaScript and Rust — 1789/1789 zero divergences on the official suite."
>
  <DocsShell
    crumb="Teptris — schema validation"
    title={`Teptris — schema validation (engine v${VERSIONS.toml})`}
    lede="libteptris validates TOML documents against JSON Schema (draft 2020-12, practical subset). Compile a schema once, validate many documents — the same engine serves C, the CLI, Ruby, Python, JavaScript and Rust. Zero divergences on the entire official test suite: 1789/1789 (2026-11-12)."
  >
    <p>
      A schema is ordinary JSON Schema — the same vocabulary you use for
      JSON today. The engine compiles it into an immutable, arena-backed
      graph and validates parsed TOML against it, collecting
      <em>every</em> violation (up to 256) rather than stopping at the
      first. The violation shape is identical on every surface:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="json"
      code={`{ "path": "server.port",       // where in the document (dotted TOML path, array indices as items.0.name)
  "schemaPath": "#/properties/server/properties/port",  // where in the schema
  "message": "expected a matching type, found string"   // why
}`}
    />
    <p>
      An empty result means the document is valid. Documents that do
      not parse raise the binding&rsquo;s parse error (line/column)
      instead — a syntax failure is never reported as a violation.
    </p>

    <h2 id="cli">The CLI</h2>
    <p>
      <code>teptris validate</code> compiles a schema file, validates a
      TOML file, prints the violation JSON array on stdout, and exits
      non-zero when anything fails:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="console"
      code={`$ teptris validate --schema service.json service.toml
[{"path":"port","schemaPath":"#/properties.port","message":"expected a matching type, found string"}]
$ echo $?   # 1

$ teptris validate --schema service.json good.toml && echo ok
ok`}
    />
    <p>
      The output is jq-pipeable — this is the same handback every
      binding decodes.
    </p>

    <h2 id="c">C</h2>
    <p>
      The stable ABI lives in <code>&lt;teptris/schema.h&gt;</code>
      (shipped by <code>cmake --install</code>). Compile, validate,
      free; every buffer handed back is <code>malloc</code>&rsquo;d
      memory you release:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="c"
      code={`#include <string.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <teptris/schema.h>

teptris_schema *sch = NULL;
char *diag = NULL;
const char *sj = "{\\"properties\\":{\\"port\\":{\\"type\\":\\"integer\\"}}}";

if (teptris_schema_compile(sj, strlen(sj), &sch, &diag, NULL) != TEPTRIS_OK) {
    fprintf(stderr, "schema rejected: %s\\n", diag ? diag : "?");
    teptris_diag_free(diag);
    return 1;
}
char *buf = NULL; size_t n = 0;
const char *doc = "port = \\"x\\"\\n";
teptris_schema_validate(sch, doc, strlen(doc), &buf, &n);
/* buf = [{"path":"port","schemaPath":"#/properties.port",
 *        "message":"expected a matching type, found string"}] */
free(buf);
teptris_schema_free(sch);`}
    />
    <Callout kind="info">
      <code>teptris_schema_compile_ex</code> takes a base directory so
      relative <code>$ref</code>s resolve against local files. The
      library never touches the network: absolute-URI references are
      rejected at compile.
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="ruby">Ruby</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="ruby"
      code={`require "teptris"

schema = Teptris::Schema.compile('{"properties":{"port":{"type":"integer"}}}')

schema.valid?("port = 8080\\n")        # => true
schema.validate("port = \\"x\\"\\n")
# => [#<Teptris::Schema::Violation path: "port",
#        schema_path: "#/properties.port",
#        message: "expected a matching type, found string">]

# each violation answers .path, .schema_path, .message (and .to_h)`}
    />
    <p>
      Compile failures raise <code>Teptris::Error</code> carrying the
      engine&rsquo;s diagnostic JSON; documents that do not parse raise
      <code>Teptris::ParseError</code> with line/column.
    </p>

    <h2 id="python">Python</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="python"
      code={`import teptris

schema = teptris.compile_schema('{"properties":{"port":{"type":"integer"}}}')

schema.valid('port = 8080\\n')     # => True
schema.validate('port = "x"\\n')
# => [{"path": "port",
#      "schema_path": "#/properties.port",
#      "message": "expected a matching type, found string"}]`}
    />
    <p>
      <code>teptris.Schema</code> accepts str or bytes; violations come
      back as plain dicts with <code>path</code> /
      <code>schema_path</code> / <code>message</code>. Malformed
      documents raise <code>TOMLDecodeError</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="javascript">JavaScript / TypeScript</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="ts"
      code={`import { init } from "teptris";

const api = await init();
const schema = api.compileSchema('{"properties":{"port":{"type":"integer"}}}');

schema.validate("port = 8080\\n");  // => []
schema.validate('port = "x"\\n');
// => [{ path: "port", schemaPath: "#/properties.port",
//       message: "expected a matching type, found string" }]`}
    />
    <p>
      The handle is wasm-side — call <code>schema.free()</code> when
      done. Compile failures throw <code>Error</code> carrying the
      diagnostic JSON.
    </p>

    <h2 id="rust">Rust</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="rust"
      code={`use teptris::schema::Schema;

let schema = Schema::compile(r#"{"properties":{"port":{"type":"integer"}}}"#).unwrap();

let violations = schema.validate(b"port = \\"x\\"\\n").unwrap();
assert_eq!(violations[0].path, "port");
assert_eq!(violations[0].schema_path, "#/properties.port");
assert!(schema.validate(b"port = 8080\\n").unwrap().is_empty());`}
    />
    <p>
      Compile failures return <code>SchemaCompileError</code> carrying
      the diagnostic verbatim. A compiled <code>Schema</code> is
      <code>Send + Sync</code> — share it across threads.
    </p>

    <h2 id="policies">The four TOML/JSON policies</h2>
    <p>
      TOML and JSON differ in exactly four places; each difference has
      a documented policy (engine contract — <code>SCHEMAS.md</code> in
      the repository):
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>null</strong> — TOML has no null. A branch requiring
        null matches no TOML value; optionality stays with
        <code>required</code> + absence, so the JSON idiom
        <code>"type": ["string", "null"]</code> behaves as an optional
        string.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>integer</strong> — TOML&rsquo;s integer is a distinct
        type from float: <code>1</code> and <code>1.0</code> differ.
        <code>"type": "integer"</code> matches only TOML integers;
        <code>"type": "number"</code> matches both.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>datetimes</strong> — TOML&rsquo;s four datetime shapes
        validate as <code>"type": "string"</code>, and the
        <code>format</code> keywords <code>date-time</code>,
        <code>date</code>, <code>time</code> check the datetime shape —
        a TOML datetime of the matching shape passes, and so does a
        string whose content parses as that shape.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>non-finite floats</strong> — <code>nan</code>,
        <code>inf</code>, <code>-inf</code> are TOML floats matching
        <code>"type": "number"</code>; nan fails every
        range/multipleOf, inf violates any <code>maximum</code>. No
        special-casing.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="subset">What compiles — and what rejects</h2>
    <p>
      The engine implements the practical 2020-12 subset: types,
      enums/consts, numeric and string constraints, required/
      properties/additionalProperties, items/prefixItems/contains,
      allOf/anyOf/oneOf/not, <code>$ref</code> (local pointers,
      <code>$anchor</code>s, recursive definitions),
      <code>$defs</code>/<code>definitions</code>, default/
      examples/deprecated/annotations, and content keywords. Unknown
      keywords are ignored (extensions, per the spec).
    </p>
    <p>
      Constructs that would <em>silently change results</em> if ignored
      are rejected at compile with a diagnostic naming the problem —
      never silently skipped:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="console"
      code={`schema.compile('{"properties":{"p":{"pattern":"((a"}}}')
# => schema rejected: {"error":"unterminated group at pattern"}`}
    />
    <p>
      That class: <code>unevaluatedProperties</code>/
      <code>unevaluatedItems</code>, <code>$dynamicRef</code>/
      <code>$dynamicAnchor</code>/
      <code>$recursiveRef</code>/<code>$recursiveAnchor</code>,
      backreferences and lookaround in patterns, and any remote
      (absolute-URI) <code>$ref</code> — a parser library never touches
      the network.
    </p>

    <h2 id="formats">Formats: annotation by default, assertion on demand</h2>
    <p>
      Draft 2020-12 makes <code>format</code> annotation-only by
      default, and teptris follows the spec: schemas that declare the
      standard 2020-12 dialect treat <code>format</code> as an
      annotation. Schemas that opt in via the sanctioned
      <a href="https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/format-assertion">
      format-assertion dialect</a>
      (<code>{`{"$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/format-assertion"}`}</code>)
      get format checking. When formats assert, the battery is real:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="text"
      code={`date, time, date-time, datetime-local, time-local   RFC 3339 (leap seconds at the UTC day end)
email, idn-email                                    RFC 5322 / RFC 6531 (quoted locals, LDH labels)
hostname, idn-hostname                              RFC 1035/5891 + UTS #46 + IDNA:
                                                      NFC, RFC 3492 punycode, RFC 5892 context rules,
                                                      RFC 5893 bidi rules, wire-form length gates
uri, uri-reference, iri, iri-reference              RFC 3986/3987 (authority, port, IPv6 literals, IRI unicode)
uri-template                                        RFC 6570 (operators, varname dots, :N / * modifiers)
uuid                                                RFC 4122 (8-4-4-4-12 hex)
json-pointer, relative-json-pointer                 RFC 6901
regex                                               the engine's own NFA (a compile trial)
duration                                            RFC 3339 duration grammar (strict canonical order)`}
    />
    <p>
      Every case above is asserted against the official JSON Schema
      test suite&rsquo;s unified model — 1789/1789, zero divergences —
      including the punycode, context-rule and bidi corners of
      internationalized hostnames.
    </p>

    <h2 id="patterns">Patterns are ReDoS-immune</h2>
    <p>
      <code>pattern</code> and <code>patternProperties</code> run on a
      linear-time NFA (Thompson construction): no backtracking, no
      pathological inputs, no host hangs. The practical ECMA subset is
      supported: literals, <code>.</code>, classes, groups,
      alternation, greedy quantifiers, anchors, <code>\b</code>,
      <code>\d \w \s</code> (and negations), <code>\xNN</code>,
      <code>\uNNNN</code>; lazy quantifiers are accepted (boolean match
      semantics are identical).
    </p>

    <Callout kind="info">
      The complete semantics — the four policies, the subset, the
      format battery — are the engine contract documented in
      <code>SCHEMAS.md</code> at
      <a href="https://github.com/leptris/teptris">leptris/teptris</a>.
    </Callout>
  </DocsShell>
</BaseLayout>
